1. CHECK THE WALL OUTLET
   Plug another device, like a lamp or phone charger, into the exact same outlet to ensure it is actually providing power. Sometimes the issue is just a tripped breaker or a dead wall socket.

2. INSPECT THE PHYSICAL CONNECTIONS

   Make sure the power cable is firmly plugged into the brick adapter, the brick is fully connected to the cord going to the wall, and the charging tip is pushed all the way into the laptop's charging port.

3. EXAMINE THE CORDS AND ADAPTER FOR DAMAGE

   Run your fingers along the length of the power cables to feel for any fraying, bends, or breaks. Smell the power brick - if you notice a faint burning odor, the internal components of the adapter may have failed.

4. CLEAR OUT THE CHARGING PORT

   Look inside the laptop's charging port using a flashlight. Dust, lint, or debris often gets trapped inside and prevents a solid connection. If you see anything, gently remove it using a wooden toothpick. Avoid metal objects like needles, which can damage the pins.

5. TEST THE BATTERY AND POWER CYCLE (HARD RESET)

   Unplug the charger from the laptop, disconnect all USB devices, and hold down the laptop's power button for a full 15 to 30 seconds to drain any residual power. After doing this, reconnect the charger and try turning it on to see if the charging indicator light responds.
